-------------------------------------------------------------------
1. the manifest drift gate
-------------------------------------------------------------------
The committed manifest must be checked BEFORE it is regenerated.
Regenerating first compares the file with itself and cannot detect drift.

NOTE on the measurement method: the tamper in this log was left in the
WORKING TREE only. It was therefore never committed, so the next
regeneration simply overwrote it -- which is why order C could not see it
here. CI faces a COMMITTED manifest that no longer matches the lockfiles, so
order C has to be measured against a COMMITTED tamper. That measurement is
in 13-manifest-drift-committed-tamper.log, where order C DOES detect the
drift. The conclusion about which order to use does not change (--check is
the order that does not write the file it is checking), but the reason is
different -- see docs/17-BUILD-CI.md §3.

--- tamper with one integrity value
tampered: "integrity": "sha512-Xz4Tpyki7XyrpbUK1jR1

--- order A (the OLD order): regenerate, then --check
exit code = 0   (0 means the drift was NOT detected)

--- order B (the NEW order): --check first
exit code = 1   (non-zero means the drift WAS detected)

--- order C: regenerate, then git diff --exit-code
exit code = 0   (0 means the drift was NOT detected)
  -> the tamper here was never COMMITTED, so regenerating overwrote it
     and the diff saw nothing. Measured against a COMMITTED tamper, order C
     exits 1 -- see 13-manifest-drift-committed-tamper.log.

    RESULT: OK -- the committed dependency manifest matches the lockfiles.
      packages=88 licenceKnown=88 reviewRequired=16
restored; --check exit code = 0

